@flexiarg contracts/rebase-by-name
@title Rebase Pointers By Name, And Refuse Ambiguity
@keywords rebase, line-numbers, named-form, drift, ambiguity
@audience contract maintainers
@tone technical
@style pattern
@status draft 2026-09-16 (claude-7; mathlib4 scripts/rebase-contract-loci.py, dd56aa89d8, extended to live call sites 7e6760dba5)
@references [contracts/resolve-pointers-at-head translation/bind-to-the-source]
@see-also hygiene/observe-the-authority
@how re-find each pointer's named form at HEAD; change a line only when the name occurs exactly once; report absence or duplication for a person. Line numbers quoted inside prose are not rebased and become stale text; keep binding pointers in structured fields.
@violation-signature a pointer moved to the nearest defn; line numbers edited by offset; a falsifier's quoted ":440" relied on after rebase.
! conclusion: Rebase pointers by their named form, only on a unique match, and keep binding line numbers out of prose.

  + context: Test files grow by tens of lines per delivery.

  + IF:
    Committed changes move pointer lines.

  + HOWEVER:
    Names can be duplicated or removed.

  + THEN:
    Rebase by unique name and report the rest.

  + BECAUSE:
    The name is the identity of the form; the line number is only where it currently sits.

  + evidence: 11 fixture deftests moved +50 lines after P10/P12 test additions, and +68 after P10 part 2, each rebased by name; prose citations such as "(sparse-g-equals-enumerating-g :440)" left stale.
